&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Verify the fix
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the dev server (&lt;code&gt;npm run dev&lt;/code&gt;) and opening the page on a Chrome device emulator showed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No horizontal scroll.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The TabBar appears at the bottom, icons highlight correctly when navigating.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The desktop layout remains untouched (&lt;code&gt;md&lt;/code&gt; breakpoint).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also added a quick Cypress test (`c&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-25&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My Surface Laptop Ultra decodes short code replies from qwen3-coder:30b at &lt;strong&gt;90.9 tokens a second&lt;/strong&gt;, twice the 44.7 that &lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/AppsonAzureBlog/qwen3-8-27b-on-a-lenovo-laptop-run-it-on-rtx-spark-watch-it-in-azure-monitor/4567828" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Microsoft's Lenovo Yoga 9n review&lt;/a&gt; measured for Qwen3.8-27B on the same chip. Both laptops use NVIDIA's RTX Spark N1X with 64 GB of LPDDR5X-9400. I repeated that post's two benchmarks with Ollama on Windows: no Docker, no vLLM, no speculative decoding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The long prompt tells a different story. With a 100,589-token prompt, qwen3-coder took 146 seconds to its first token and then decoded at 19.7 tokens a second, where the Lenovo setup held 44.3. Over the whole run the GPU averaged 33 W and peaked at 75 °C.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  At a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict:&lt;/strong&gt; with Ollama alone, the Surface Laptop Ultra is a fast machine for short, interactive coding requests with a local model. For a coding agent that resends a 100K-token conversation on every turn, the Lenovo post's vLLM setup is the better pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Setup is one signed installer and one &lt;code&gt;ollama pull&lt;/code&gt;. Nothing runs in WSL or Docker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;qwen3-coder:30b (30.5B parameters, 8 of 128 experts active per token) and a 102,400-token context fit entirely on the GPU: 26.7 GiB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;90.9 tokens a second on short code prompts, steady across three runs (88–92).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;qwen3:8b, the smaller model, decodes at 44–50 tokens a second in every run I made.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Good to know:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decode speed falls about 4.6 times between a 20-token and a 100K-token prompt, because the attention cache grows with every token.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prompt processing ran at 687 tokens a second at 100K tokens, two-thirds of the Lenovo setup's 1,033.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ollama silently cuts a prompt longer than its context window to about half. Only its server log says so.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who it's for:&lt;/strong&gt; developers who want a local coding model for chat, edits and short agent turns, with no per-token bill and no container stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The machine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Surface Laptop Ultra has the same chip and memory as the Lenovo in Microsoft's post, so the hardware comparison is like for like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Microsoft Surface Laptop Ultra, as tested&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NVIDIA RTX Spark N1X: 20-core Arm CPU, 6,144-core Blackwell GPU, plus an NVIDIA NPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;64 GB of Samsung LPDDR5X-9400, shared by the CPU and the GPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GPU's share&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31,232 MiB dedicated to the GPU; Windows sees the other 32.2 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Software&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Windows 11 Pro on Arm (build 28000), NVIDIA driver 617.42, Ollama 0.40.11 (native Windows ARM64, per-user install)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Power&lt;/td&gt;
