&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GPU's share is the same 32 GB split the Lenovo post used. With qwen3-coder and its 102,400-token cache loaded, GPU memory peaked at 29,952 of 31,232 MiB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How I measured
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I copied the Lenovo post's two single-request benchmarks as closely as Ollama allows; the agent session and Azure Monitor parts I left out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Code benchmark&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Long-prompt benchmark&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Task&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A thread-safe LRU cache in Python with tests, a token-bucket rate limiter in TypeScript, Dijkstra's algorithm in Go&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One question after a long prompt cut from Windows PowerShell's own format and module files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18–21 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,589 tokens (qwen3-coder) or 37,151 (qwen3:8b, whose limit is 40,960), with a random first line so nothing came from the prompt cache&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reply&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;512 tokens each, temperature 0, seed 42, thinking off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;384 tokens, temperature 0, thinking off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;102,400 tokens (qwen3-coder), 40,960 (qwen3:8b)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How it was sent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One request at a time to Ollama's &lt;code&gt;/api/generate&lt;/code&gt;, after a warm-up load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One request, after a calibration prompt that measured characters per token&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The script, &lt;code&gt;speed-bench.ps1&lt;/code&gt;, unloads every model first, records Ollama's own token timings and samples GPU power, temperature, clock and memory with &lt;code&gt;nvidia-smi&lt;/code&gt; once a second. Decode speed is Ollama's &lt;code&gt;eval_count / eval_duration&lt;/code&gt;; prompt speed is &lt;code&gt;prompt_eval_count / prompt_eval_duration&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One lesson from the first attempt: my first long prompt for qwen3-coder came out at 105,530 tokens, over its 102,400-token window, and Ollama kept only the last 51,202 without returning an error. The script now calibrates on a large slice of the text and flags any prompt that comes back short.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Results
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On short prompts, qwen3-coder:30b is the fastest setup on either laptop; at 100K tokens it is the slowest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcbzbccxrz1xqdyk5xeon.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcbzbccxrz1xqdyk5xeon.png" alt="Decode speed: qwen3-coder 90.9 tok/s short, 19.7 at 100,589 tokens; qwen3:8b 45.1 short, 22.3 at 37,151 tokens; Qwen3.8-27B on vLLM 44.7 short, 44.3 at 97,880 tokens" width="800" height="412"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three code prompts landed close together: 92.1, 90.2 and 90.5 tokens a second for qwen3-coder, and 46.5, 45.1 and 43.8 for qwen3:8b. The first token of a short reply arrived in 0.08–0.19 seconds once the model was loaded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading the long prompt is where the wait comes from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Setup&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Prompt&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Prompt processing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;First token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, Ollama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,589 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;687 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;146 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, Ollama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;37,151 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,492 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Qwen3.8-27B, vLLM on the Lenovo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;97,880 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,033 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;about 95 s, worked out from its prompt speed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a chat that grows turn by turn, Ollama reuses the cached start of the conversation, so only the new part is read. The 146 seconds is the cost of a brand-new 100K-token prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Power, heat and clocks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GPU slows its clock while it reads a long prompt and speeds back up to reply, so the lower clock tracks the work, not heat building up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvu1abveup331v88ivec.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvu1abveup331v88ivec.png" alt="GPU power, temperature and graphics clock over the qwen3-coder run: about 34 W and 1,656 MHz while reading the 100K prompt, 2,260 MHz while replying, 60 W and 75 °C peaks" width="799" height="521"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading a prompt is compute-bound: after a short burst to 60 W, the GPU settled near 34 W and dropped its clock to about 1.65 GHz for the whole 146 seconds. Replying is memory-bound: the clock went back up to about 2.25 GHz at 30 W, and the temperature fell from 71 to 64 °C. That pattern looks like a power limit, not thermal throttling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 75 °C peak is 8 °C above the Lenovo's three-hour peak, reached in a run of about four minutes. I haven't tested sustained load yet, so treat this as a first look at the Surface's cooling, not a verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why long context costs so much here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Decode on this chip is a question of bytes, as the Lenovo post showed: every token reads the active weights plus the whole attention cache, at roughly 235 GB/s.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;tokens per second ≈ memory bandwidth ÷ bytes read per token
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;qwen3-coder's mixture of experts makes the weights cheap: only about 3.3B of its 30.5B parameters run per token, about 2 GB at Q4_K_M. Its cache is the expensive part. Each token keeps keys and values for 48 layers × 4 heads × 128 dimensions in 16-bit floats, 96 KiB a token, so 100,000 tokens of cache is about 9.9 GB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Run&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Weights read per token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cache read per token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Measured decode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Implied bandwidth&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, short prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2.0 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.05 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;90.9 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~185 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, 100,589-token prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2.0 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~9.9 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19.7 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, short prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.1 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.08 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;45.1 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, 37,151-token prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.1 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.5 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;22.3 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three of the four runs imply the same 235 GB/s, close to the 229 GB/s the Lenovo post measured with a read test. The short qwen3-coder run falls short of it because routing tokens between experts adds work that isn't memory reads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weights and cache sizes are my estimates from the model files and their architecture, not measurements. They explain the gap with the Lenovo setup: Qwen3.8-27B keeps a fixed-size state in 48 of its 64 layers, so its cache barely grows, and speculative decoding produces about five tokens per read of the weights.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Laptop vs laptop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hardware matches, so the differences come from the software stack and the model. Read this as two working setups, not a controlled benchmark.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Surface Laptop Ultra (this post)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Lenovo Yoga 9n 2-in-1 (&lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/AppsonAzureBlog/qwen3-8-27b-on-a-lenovo-laptop-run-it-on-rtx-spark-watch-it-in-azure-monitor/4567828" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Microsoft's post&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
